Lucent @VERSION@: corresponding source of the LGPL libraries
=

This archive is the complete corresponding source of the libraries that Lucent @VERSION@
(Lucent-Setup-@VERSION@-x64.exe) contains under the GNU Lesser General Public License, as they were
built for that installer: the upstream source archives, the build recipes with every patch
(including the Lucent project's own changes) and the build settings. It is published next to the
installer, from the same place and for as long as the installer is offered.

Lucent's own code is free software under the MIT License. You do not need it to rebuild or replace
these libraries: Lucent loads them dynamically (see "How Lucent uses them" and "Lucent's own
source").

Contents
--------

README.txt          this file
MANIFEST.json       every library with its licence, the files Lucent ships from it and what loads
                    them, its source archives (download location, size, SHA-512, SHA-256, the
                    recipe file that pins each one), its recipe files (SHA-256) and its build
                    features; the triplet; and the build's other ports with the hashes of their
                    sources (not included here, see "notIncluded")
SHA256SUMS.txt      the SHA-256 of every other file in this archive
sources\            the upstream source archives, byte for byte as the build used them
ports\<port>\       the vcpkg build recipe ("port") of each library: portfile.cmake, the patches,
                    vcpkg.json. A recipe is either vcpkg's port at the registry commit named below,
                    unchanged, or the Lucent project's overlay of it; the libraries named below say
                    which. Every patch a recipe names (portfile.cmake applies it to the library's
                    upstream source; a few only on other platforms or with other features, as the
                    portfile says) is listed with that library ("Modified"): the files it changes,
                    and who made it and when (vcpkg's patches as they are in its registry at that
                    commit; the Lucent project's own, named lucent-*.patch, with the date each
                    states; they also mark the files they change). So is each script of a recipe
                    that portfile.cmake runs on the library's files before the build (GNU libintl's
                    bashify.cmake rewrites its configure script). Other steps of a recipe, such as
                    copying build files into the source tree or rewriting the files the build
                    generates, are in its portfile.cmake
triplets\           the vcpkg triplet @TRIPLET@ (the build settings): 64-bit x64, shared
                    libraries (DLLs), the dynamic Microsoft C runtime (/MD), release builds only,
                    and the linker and compiler options that keep the build machine's folders out of
                    the binaries
build\<port>\       vcpkg_abi_info.txt: vcpkg's record of what went into each build (its features,
                    the hashes of its recipe files, the triplet and vcpkg's helper scripts, and the
                    tool versions)
lucent\             Lucent's vcpkg.json and vcpkg-configuration.json: the registry commit, the
                    features Lucent asks for, and its overlay folders (cmake\ports and
                    cmake\triplets in Lucent's source are ports\ and triplets\ here)
licenses\           vcpkg-LICENSE.txt: vcpkg's licence, the MIT License
                    (@VCPKG_COPYRIGHT@), LICENSE.txt of the vcpkg registry at
                    the commit named below; it covers vcpkg's recipes in ports\.
                    Lucent-LICENSE.txt: Lucent's licence, the MIT License
                    (@LUCENT_COPYRIGHT@); it covers the Lucent project's overlay ports
                    and patches, triplets\ and lucent\@LICENCE_TEXTS@

When this archive was made, every file in sources\ was checked: its SHA-512 is the one vcpkg
verified before building the library, and the one the library's recipe pins (for vcpkg's own
ports, in the recipe at the registry commit). To check a file yourself:

    certutil -hashfile <file> SHA512
    (or in PowerShell: Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA512 <file>)

The libraries
-------------

@COMPONENTS@
@BUILD_TOOLS@
How Lucent uses them
--------------------

Lucent loads every library above dynamically. Each one is a separate DLL in Lucent's installation
folder (by default %LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\Lucent). When a program starts, Windows loads the DLLs it
imports from that folder by file name, and then the DLLs those import, in the same way:

@LOADING@

@STATIC_CODE@

While Lucent runs, Qt also loads its plugins (plugins\) and the plugins of the QML modules Lucent
uses (qml\) by file name. Lucent does not check signatures or hashes of these DLLs and does
nothing to prevent you from replacing them.

Rebuilding a library
--------------------

@BUILT_WITH@

1. Install Visual Studio 2022, or its Build Tools, with "Desktop development with C++" (the MSVC
   v143 x64 toolset and a Windows SDK), and Git for Windows.
2. Get vcpkg at that commit, in a short folder: Qt's build needs short paths unless Windows' long
   paths are enabled.

       git clone https://github.com/microsoft/vcpkg C:\src\vcpkg
       git -C C:\src\vcpkg checkout @BASELINE@
       C:\src\vcpkg\bootstrap-vcpkg.bat -disableMetrics

3. Extract this archive into C:\src, which creates C:\src\@ROOT@.
4. Copy the files of sources\ into C:\src\vcpkg\downloads, so that vcpkg uses them instead of
   downloading them; it checks each one against the SHA-512 in its recipe. vcpkg still downloads its
   own tools (CMake, Ninja, MSYS2 and others) and the sources of the libraries' dependencies that
   are not in this archive; MANIFEST.json lists those with their hashes.
5. Build, from an "x64 Native Tools Command Prompt for VS 2022":

@INSTALL_COMMAND@

   --overlay-ports makes vcpkg build with the recipes of this archive, --overlay-triplets with its
   triplet, and the names in brackets are the features each library was built with (also in
   MANIFEST.json and build\<port>\vcpkg_abi_info.txt). You can build one library alone the same
   way, for example @ONE_LIBRARY@.
6. The DLLs are in C:\src\vcpkg\installed\@TRIPLET@\bin.

To change a library, change its source and build it again: add a patch to ports\<port>\ and list it
in the PATCHES of its portfile.cmake (as the lucent-*.patch files are), or run "vcpkg install" with
--editable, which keeps the extracted source in C:\src\vcpkg\buildtrees\<port>\src for you to edit
before you build again.

Replacing a library in Lucent
-----------------------------

Close Lucent, then copy your DLL over the file of the same name in Lucent's installation folder
(keep the original, to go back). Your build must stay binary compatible with the one Lucent was
built against: 64-bit x64, built with Microsoft Visual C++ against the dynamic C runtime, the same
version of the library and the same configuration (features and options). Replace Qt's files only
with a build of the same Qt version and configuration: Qt's modules and plugins use each other's
internal interfaces. Repairing or updating Lucent with its installer puts its own files back.

Lucent's own source
-------------------

@LUCENT_SOURCE@

Licences
--------

Each library keeps its own licence. Its texts are in its source archive where that archive holds
them. Where it does not (a licence the archive names without its text, or code of others inside the
library under a licence whose text the archive lacks), the text is in another archive here, in
licenses\ here (listed under Contents), or in Lucent's installation folder, whose
licenses\THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt lists each part of a library under a licence of its own with the
file of its notice; each such file says where its licence's text comes from. For example, the
LGPL-2.1 of the TRE code inside Lensfun is not in Lensfun's archive (LibRaw's archive here holds it,
as LICENSE.LGPL), and the MIT text of LibRaw's Adobe DNG SDK code is not in LibRaw's archive
(Lucent's installation folder holds it, as nlohmann-json's licence). licenses\LGPL-NOTICE.txt in
that folder names this archive. vcpkg's recipes are under the MIT License
(@VCPKG_COPYRIGHT@; licenses\vcpkg-LICENSE.txt in this archive). The Lucent
project's overlay ports, patches and triplet are under the MIT License
(@LUCENT_COPYRIGHT@; licenses\Lucent-LICENSE.txt in this archive). A patch's own lines
are under its author's licence (vcpkg's or the Lucent project's MIT License); the lines it quotes
and changes stay under the licences of the files they come from, which MANIFEST.json records for
each patch (components[].modifications[]: licence, quotes). A library built with patches remains
under its own licence.
